1 claims; 0 removal(s).
  run_ids: ["758c8da6-af9a-48dc-9593-c8c3ca461f79"] -> ["f65c29f2-61b7-4615-9208-0cf5ad4d4a9c"]
  explanation: "For rand_xorshift 0.5.0, the Aeneas proof establishes the exact try_next_u32 word and state update for every four-u32 state, using three explicit std wrapping-operator models. With t=x XOR (x << 11), r=w XOR (w >> 19) XOR t XOR (t >> 8); the returned value is Ok(r) and state becomes (y,z,w,r), using 32-bit logical operations. A separate Lean theorem derives preservation of a nonzero state from this exact transition. Independently, Kani checks the unchanged from_seed body for every 16-byte seed: four little-endian u32 words are decoded, and the all-zero input is replaced by four 0x0BAD5EED words. The constructed state is nonzero. A second Kani harness checks that the original next_u32 body preserves every nonzero four-word state. These seed and preservation checks are supplementary Kani results, not an Aeneas seed-body claim. No original Rust source or manifest is changed. A further original-source Kani check covers every 16-byte seed, including the zero-seed fallback: from_seed followed by try_next_u64 returns the same little-endian low/high word composition as two try_next_u32 calls on an identical clone, and derived equality confirms every resulting state word agrees. This is a public-API relational guarantee over seed-constructed states, not an independent recurrence specification. Separate Kani also executes the original manual Debug formatter for every public from_seed input or seed_from_u64 input. With default formatting and a fixed64-byte writer it emits exactly \"XorShiftRng {}\" (13bytes), preserves the zero suffix and correct length; a writer rejecting the first write instead propagates fmt::Error with no bytes retained. A separate original try_fill_bytes relation is checked for every16-byte seed, every initial8-byte array and every requested prefix length0..8. Filling returns Ok, writes exactly the little-endian prefix from ceil(length/4) actual next32 calls, preserves the unused array suffix and yields an equal complete final generator state. Separate original from_rng/try_from_rng Kani relations cover concrete source protocols: every nonzero16-byte seed, optionally preceded by one all-zero block, produces the same complete state as public from_seed after exactly1or2 source fills. Fallible sources additionally propagate every u32 error payload after zero or one discarded zero block, preserving the expected call count even if the failing fill first writes arbitrary bytes. With the serde feature, a separate Kani check calls original Serialize for every public 16-byte seed through a fixed serializer: it emits the XorShiftRng struct with x/y/z/w u32 fields in order, matching little-endian seed words with the documented all-zero fallback, and leaves the RNG state unchanged. A failure at any of its ten serializer callback stages propagates the exact supplied u32 error and stops at that stage." -> "For rand_xorshift 0.5.0, the Aeneas proof establishes the exact try_next_u32 word and state update for every four-u32 state, using three explicit std wrapping-operator models. With t=x XOR (x << 11), r=w XOR (w >> 19) XOR t XOR (t >> 8); the returned value is Ok(r) and state becomes (y,z,w,r), using 32-bit logical operations. A separate Lean theorem derives preservation of a nonzero state from this exact transition. Independently, Kani checks the unchanged from_seed body for every 16-byte seed: four little-endian u32 words are decoded, and the all-zero input is replaced by four 0x0BAD5EED words. The constructed state is nonzero. A second Kani harness checks that the original next_u32 body preserves every nonzero four-word state. These seed and preservation checks are supplementary Kani results, not an Aeneas seed-body claim. No original Rust source or manifest is changed. A further original-source Kani check covers every 16-byte seed, including the zero-seed fallback: from_seed followed by try_next_u64 returns the same little-endian low/high word composition as two try_next_u32 calls on an identical clone, and derived equality confirms every resulting state word agrees. This is a public-API relational guarantee over seed-constructed states, not an independent recurrence specification. Separate Kani also executes the original manual Debug formatter for every public from_seed input or seed_from_u64 input. With default formatting and a fixed64-byte writer it emits exactly \"XorShiftRng {}\" (13bytes), preserves the zero suffix and correct length; a writer rejecting the first write instead propagates fmt::Error with no bytes retained. A separate original try_fill_bytes relation is checked for every16-byte seed, every initial8-byte array and every requested prefix length0..8. Filling returns Ok, writes exactly the little-endian prefix from ceil(length/4) actual next32 calls, preserves the unused array suffix and yields an equal complete final generator state. Separate original from_rng/try_from_rng Kani relations cover concrete source protocols: every nonzero16-byte seed, optionally preceded by one all-zero block, produces the same complete state as public from_seed after exactly1or2 source fills. Fallible sources additionally propagate every u32 error payload after zero or one discarded zero block, preserving the expected call count even if the failing fill first writes arbitrary bytes. With the serde feature, a separate Kani check calls original Serialize for every public 16-byte seed through a fixed serializer: it emits the XorShiftRng struct with x/y/z/w u32 fields in order, matching little-endian seed words with the documented all-zero fallback, and leaves the RNG state unchanged. A failure at any of its ten serializer callback stages propagates the exact supplied u32 error and stops at that stage. A separate Kani check calls original Deserialize through a fixed sequence deserializer for every 16-byte wire value and lengths zero through four. Lengths below four return the concrete invalid_length error for that length; a supplied error at any reached callback stage propagates unchanged. Successful four-word deserialization, including all-zero state, serializes through the fixed serializer to the exact original 16 bytes and leaves that state unchanged."
  trusted_assumptions: "The Rust compiler, Charon/Aeneas translation, existing Aeneas scalar primitives, Lean kernel, and Kani/CBMC toolchains are trusted. Three executable std Wrapping<u32> models connect shift-left, shift-right, and xor to existing Aeneas primitives. Kani checks their Rust correspondence for all u32 operands and all 64-bit usize shift amounts; the relation between these Rust formulas and the Lean definitions remains an explicitly reviewed semantic bridge, not an in-Lean proof of the std bodies. No extra local axiom occurs in the public theorem's transitive dependencies. The seed and preservation harnesses embed the entire original Rust implementation as an unchanged prefix, appending safe observers in a std verification module. This preserves the implementation text but retains the documented verification-module and compiler/toolchain boundary. The separate Nonzero.lean proof uses the existing Aeneas exact transition theorem, not an assumed new seed theorem. This Serialize supplement trusts Kani/CBMC, the August Rust compiler and the exact rand_core 0.10.0 / serde 1.0.228 implementations; its finite callback serializer is the observed protocol, not an assumption about arbitrary serializers." -> "The Rust compiler, Charon/Aeneas translation, existing Aeneas scalar primitives, Lean kernel, and Kani/CBMC toolchains are trusted. Three executable std Wrapping<u32> models connect shift-left, shift-right, and xor to existing Aeneas primitives. Kani checks their Rust correspondence for all u32 operands and all 64-bit usize shift amounts; the relation between these Rust formulas and the Lean definitions remains an explicitly reviewed semantic bridge, not an in-Lean proof of the std bodies. No extra local axiom occurs in the public theorem's transitive dependencies. The seed and preservation harnesses embed the entire original Rust implementation as an unchanged prefix, appending safe observers in a std verification module. This preserves the implementation text but retains the documented verification-module and compiler/toolchain boundary. The separate Nonzero.lean proof uses the existing Aeneas exact transition theorem, not an assumed new seed theorem. This Serialize supplement trusts Kani/CBMC, the August Rust compiler and the exact rand_core 0.10.0 / serde 1.0.228 implementations; its finite callback serializer is the observed protocol, not an assumption about arbitrary serializers. The Deserialize supplement additionally uses the concrete sequence access, DeserializeSeed and u32 visitor implementations with the same pinned Kani/Rust/serde dependencies. Its invalid_length handler returns the specified concrete error."
